# The visit counts pin the echo doctrine both ways: driving the real
# switcher emits section_selected (visits rise) while the guest's own
# programmatic select_section (button#0) does not (visits hold).
expect_sections 2
# THE ARM THAT RENDERED, never the declared prop (the expect_split
# rule): the desktops' horizontal spelling and the phones' bottom bar
# are one "bar" answer, so this line runs everywhere.
expect_sections_presentation "bar"
# THE SYMBOLS THE ROWS REALLY DRAW, not the ones the guest declared —
# the assertion whose absence let a garbage decode ship green on five
# lanes (docs/chrome/sections-symbol.md). Above the phone cut
# deliberately, since a symbol is what a bottom bar is made of; the
# SIDEBAR rows below carry none, because GTK's GtkStackSidebar binds
# only the page title (DESIGN.md, Stated platform divergences).
expect_section_symbol "Feed" "home"
expect_section_symbol "Archive" "star"
expect_section "Feed"
expect label#0 "feed ready"
select_section 1
expect_section "Archive"
expect label#1 "archive: 1 visits"
select_section 0
expect_section "Feed"
click button#0
expect_section "Archive"
expect label#1 "archive: 1 visits"
select_section 0
select_section 1
expect label#1 "archive: 2 visits"
# THE SIDEBAR ARM, in an aux window, so one scene covers BOTH values of
# the presentation enum. The phone runners cut here: their host rejects
# create_window by capability.
expect_windows 1
click button#1
expect_windows 2
expect_title window#1 "library"
expect_sections_presentation window#1 "sidebar"
